EXT. ULTRAMARATHON AID STATION, MOUNTAIN PASS - NIGHT

A folding table under a tarp. Headlamps bob in the dark. A thermos steams. A radio sits on a camp chair, its antenna taped to a tent pole.

BERNARD OYELARAN, 60s, volunteer, parka zipped to the chin, fills paper cups with broth. He hums.

SIPHO DLAMINI, 30s, jogs into the circle of light in a reflective vest, number 112 pinned to his shirt. He is breathing easily. He stops at the table and takes a cup.

BERNARD
Evening, runner. How are the legs?

SIPHO
Honestly? Perfect.

BERNARD
That's the broth. The broth cures everything.

SIPHO
It's a good broth.

BERNARD
Name and bib?

SIPHO
Sipho Dlamini, one-twelve.

Bernard checks his clipboard. He frowns. Then smiles, and ticks a box.

BERNARD
Got you. You're fourth.

SIPHO
Fourth.

BERNARD
Eight minutes behind the leader.

SIPHO
I'll catch her.

The radio crackles. Both men glance at it.

DISPATCH (O.S.)
(a woman's voice, crisp)
Station Nine, this is Control. Come in, please.

Bernard picks up the handset.

BERNARD
Control, Station Nine. Go ahead.

DISPATCH (O.S.)
Bernard, I'm showing runner one-twelve on the shuttle manifest at mile thirty-one. Do you have him?

Bernard looks at Sipho. Sipho finishes his broth. He sets the cup down, gently, as if putting down a gift.

BERNARD
(into radio)
Control, say again?

DISPATCH (O.S.)
One-twelve took the shuttle from thirty-one to thirty-eight. He's flagged for disqualification. Do you copy?

Bernard lowers the handset. The wind pulls at the tarp.

SIPHO
It was cold.

BERNARD
Son.

SIPHO
My knee went.

BERNARD
Your knee went in the car?

Sipho laughs without humor. He unpins the number from his shirt and puts it on the table. Then he reaches for a second cup of broth.

SIPHO
May I?

BERNARD
Take it.
